    description: "WHY THIS EXISTS. The owner's 2026-08-23 ruling rewrote the bar on INV-fibis and\nINV-nuhun to \"a clean verdict must NAME WHAT IT COULD NOT EXAMINE, AND AT WHAT\nANALYSIS FIDELITY\". The first half shipped (PR #251 boundary caveats, PR #482\ntaint caveats). The second half does not exist at all, and it is the reason\nNEITHER ITEM IS CLOSED. This item is that half, filed separately so the two\ninvariants close against a shipped capability rather than against a promise.\n\nCODE FACT, read at dev 7d5886fe78, not inferred:\n  * `Verdict` (verify_claims.py:791-820) has eleven fields -- claim_id,\n    claim_text, verdict, evidence_count, details, evidence, excluded_flows,\n    flow_origins, analysis_methods, sanitized_flows, caveats. NONE of them\n    names the analyzer that produced the edges the verdict rests on.\n  * `BoundaryCoverage` (verify_claims.py:823-905) models three blind spots\n    (no call edges at all; a supported language with zero; an uncatalogued\n    external module) plus two disclosure populations. EVERY ONE is a property\n    of the EDGE SET. None is \"which pass produced these edges\".\n  * `_call_production_coverage`'s own docstring (verify_claims.py:2378-2384)\n    says coverage is derived from call-edge production and explicitly NOT from\n    \"the bare fact that an analyzer pass ran (`analysis_runs` records that a\n    pass executed, not that it produced the call structure io_boundary needs)\".\n    That reasoning is correct FOR COVERAGE and is not being reopened -- but it\n    means the AnalysisRun data is deliberately unconsulted here and no other\n    consumer reports it either.\n  * The raw material EXISTS and is load-bearing elsewhere: `Edge.origin` is a\n    list of pass IDs and `Edge.__post_init__` HARD-RAISES on an empty origin or\n    origin_run_id (ir.py:110, 448-457, 577-578). verify_claims reads `src` /\n    `dst` / `type` off the raw edge dicts and never reads `origin`.\n\nMEASURED, two arms, same crate, differing ONLY in backend. Fixture and logs:\n`~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/rust_fidelity_probe_08232026/` (armA.log, armB.log,\nsurvey.armA.json, survey.armB.json, edge_diff_A_vs_B.txt).\n\n  ARM A (tree-sitter)        ARM B (--backend rust-analyzer)\n  16 nodes / 16 edges        26 nodes / 25 edges\n  0 origin=scip              10 scip nodes, 6 scip edges\n  -> THE ARM DEMONSTRABLY TOOK; the CLI's own \"installed but NOT enabled\" vs\n     \"backend ACTIVE\" warning agreed with the node counts in both arms.\n\n  VERDICT BLOCK: BYTE-IDENTICAL ACROSS THE TWO ARMS.\n    x [CONTROL-FSREAD] violated, 1 fs_read chain      (control fired, both arms)\n    ? [NET-SEND]  inconclusive -- \"calls into 3 module(s) that the I/O catalog\n                  could not classify (std, std.io, std.net)\"\n    ? [FS-WRITE]  inconclusive -- same three modules, same sentence\n    \"1 VIOLATED, 2 INCONCLUSIVE (of 3 claim(s))\" in both.\n\n  So a reader holding the verdict CANNOT TELL which analyzer produced it. The\n  only textual difference between the two runs is the partial-install nudge,\n  which is a warning about the NEXT run, not a property of THIS verdict, and is\n  suppressed entirely once a trust decision is recorded (ADR-0045).\n\nHONEST LIMIT ON THAT MEASUREMENT, stated rather than left for a reader to find:\non THIS fixture the SCIP pass contributed `references` and `contains` edges, not\n`calls`, so verdict-invariance here does not prove the verdict would ignore a\nfidelity difference that DID move call edges. It proves the weaker and\nsufficient thing this item is about: the verdict carries no fidelity signal, so\nthe reader cannot distinguish \"examined at SCIP fidelity\" from \"examined by\ntree-sitter\" from \"rust-analyzer installed and silently not used\".\n\nWHAT IS OWED (requirement, not design -- the design decision is part of the\nwork):\n  1. A verdict must report the analysis fidelity behind it: at minimum the set\n     of pass IDs / analyzers that produced the call edges the verdict rests on,\n     per language.\n  2. It must distinguish INSTALLED-BUT-OFF from ABSENT for backends that have a\n     higher-fidelity option, because those two produce identical verdicts today\n     and a reader would act differently on them.\n  3. The caveat/disclosure text must be able to say the fidelity, since that is\n     literally the second half of the ruling's sentence.\n\nCANDIDATE HOMES, and one trap. `Verdict.analysis_methods` already exists and is\na per-verdict method breakdown (ddg / ddg_mixed / structural). IT IS NOT THIS\nSIGNAL and must not be quietly widened into it: it records HOW THE TAINT WALK\nREASONED, not WHICH ANALYZER PRODUCED THE EDGES -- a `structural` verdict and a\ntree-sitter verdict are different facts. Its SHAPE is nevertheless the right\nprecedent (a per-verdict dict alongside the verdict), and the envelope has\nabsorbed sibling fields before (flow_origins at envelope 1.3). Whatever lands,\nit needs an envelope version bump and the ONE-HOME rule: one function every\nverdict path routes through, not a field each arm fills in for itself.\n\nRELATED, NOT BLOCKING:\n  * WI-zabus -- SCIP symbols carry the bare literal origin 'scip' with no\n    AnalysisRun join. That WEAKENS the join but does not block this item: edges\n    already carry origin='scip' and that is enough to answer \"which analyzer\".\n    Doing WI-zabus first would make the reported provenance a real pass ID\n    instead of a bare literal.\n  * WI-gojum -- multi-fidelity RECONCILIATION (ADR-0012 Steps 2-3, parked).\n    That item is about which edge WINS when two fidelities disagree. This item\n    is about REPORTING which fidelities ran, and is meaningful precisely\n    BECAUSE reconciliation does not happen: the two edge sets coexist today.\n  * WI-nanom -- pyright/SCIP for Python. Same shape as rust-analyzer, and it\n    will land on this path. Building the reporting before it arrives means the\n    Python backend does not need its own second home for the same fact.\n\nNOT MEASURED: whether any repo's VERDICT (not edge set) actually changes\nbetween tree-sitter and a fidelity backend; the Python side at all (WI-nanom is\nnot built); how many envelope consumers would need updating for a new field."  # 90a1
    fields: {}  # 90a1
